NAME
       git - track changes to files

SYNOPSIS
       git <command> [arguments]

DESCRIPTION
       Keeps the history of a directory the way git does, in the same
       object format, so a repository made here can be read, checked
       and cloned by git on another machine.

COMMANDS
       init [directory]
              Start a repository. Without an argument it uses the
              current directory.

       add <file>...
              Stage files for the next commit. Each file is stored as
              an object straight away, so staging is what fixes the
              content, not committing.

       rm <file>...
              Take files out of the staging area. The files themselves
              are left alone.

       status
              Which branch this is, what is staged, and whether a
              staged file has changed since it was staged.

       commit -m <message>
              Record everything staged. The author comes from
              GIT_AUTHOR, or is 'Cervus user <user@cervus>'.

       log    The commit leading here and every commit before it.

       show <object>, cat-file <hash>
              Print an object. A tree prints as one line per entry.

       hash-object [-w] <file>
              The name git would give the file. With -w it is also
              written into the repository.

       ls-files
              What is staged, with mode and hash.

OBJECTS
       Objects live in .git/objects, named by the SHA-1 of their
       contents with a header, and stored zlib-compressed - the same
       as git. Refs are files under .git/refs holding a hash, and
       .git/HEAD names the branch in use.

       The staging area is .git/index.txt, one line per file. Git's own
       index is a binary format this does not write, so git elsewhere
       will not see the staging area, only the commits.

ENVIRONMENT
       GIT_AUTHOR
              Name and address for commits, as 'Name <address>'.

EXAMPLE
         git init
         echo 'hello' > readme.txt
         git add readme.txt
         git commit -m 'first commit'
         git log

SEE ALSO
       sha1(3), inflate(3), ssh(1)
